[Event "First American Chess Congress, New York (final match, game 6)"]
[Site "New York"]
[Date "1857"]
[Round "6"]
[White "Louis Paulsen"]
[Black "Paul Morphy"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "C48"]
[Themes "queen-sacrifice,attack,development,classic"]
[Level "2"]
[Opening "Four Knights Game: Spanish Variation, Classical Variation"]
[Why "Morphy's queen sacrifice 17...Qxf3!! is the classic proof that open lines and active pieces outweigh a queen. Paulsen spends tempi on the queenside (12.c3, 13.b4, 14.a4) while every black piece aims at g2. The game also carries a famous footnote: 22...Rg2! (Steinitz) would have mated one move faster; engine confirms (#3 against #4 for the game move), so students should calculate the whole line, not stop at the first winning idea."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bb5 Bc5 5. O-O O-O 6. Nxe5 { Key: White wins nothing and loses time; Black's rook already sits on the open e-file. } 6... Re8 7. Nxc6 dxc6 8. Bc4 b5 9. Be2 Nxe4 10. Nxe4 Rxe4 11. Bf3 Re6 12. c3 Qd3 { Key: The queen settles on d3 and paralyses White's queenside development. } 13. b4 Bb6 14. a4 bxa4 15. Qxa4 Bd7 16. Ra2 Rae8 17. Qa6 Qxf3 { Key: Queen for bishop and the g-file; the threat is ...Bg2+ and ...Bxf3#. } 18. gxf3 Rg6+ 19. Kh1 Bh3 20. Rd1 Bg2+ 21. Kg1 Bxf3+ 22. Kf1 { Key: Morphy repeats; 22...Rg2! mates in 3 (23.Qd3 Rxf2+ 24.Kg1 Rg2+ 25.Kf1 Rg1#). } 22... Bg2+ 23. Kg1 Bh3+ 24. Kh1 Bxf2 { Key: Material is level again and the rook on the seventh decides; 28...Be3 and ...Rxh2+ / ...Rg2# cannot be stopped. } 25. Qf1 Bxf1 26. Rxf1 Re2 27. Ra1 Rh6 28. d4 Be3 0-1

[Event "London (casual game)"]
[Site "London"]
[Date "1858"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Henry Bird"]
[Black "Paul Morphy"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "C41"]
[Themes "attack,sacrifice,opposite-castling,calculation"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "Philidor Defense: Philidor Countergambit, Zukertort Variation"]
[Why "An attack on a king castled long. The exchange sacrifice 17...Rxf2! and the stunning 18...Qa3!! (19.bxa3 Bxa3#, engine-confirmed) show the standard tools against queenside castling: the a- and b-pawns are the targets and a queen on a3 is the battering ram. The finish is a model king hunt with queen and bishop against a king in the open."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 d6 3. d4 f5 { Key: The Philidor Counter-Gambit, Morphy's choice for sharp play. } 4. Nc3 fxe4 5. Nxe4 d5 6. Ng3 e4 7. Ne5 Nf6 8. Bg5 Bd6 9. Nh5 O-O 10. Qd2 Qe8 11. g4 { Key: Bird's pawn thrust opens lines for Black, not for White. } 11... Nxg4 12. Nxg4 Qxh5 13. Ne5 Nc6 14. Be2 Qh3 15. Nxc6 bxc6 16. Be3 Rb8 17. O-O-O { Key: The queen cannot be taken: 19.bxa3 Bxa3# (mate in 1). White must allow 19...Qxa2. } 17... Rxf2 18. Bxf2 Qa3 19. c3 Qxa2 20. b4 Qa1+ 21. Kc2 Qa4+ 22. Kb2 Bxb4 { Key: The last defenders are stripped away; Black has queen and bishop against rooks that are not in play. } 23. cxb4 Rxb4+ 24. Qxb4 Qxb4+ 25. Kc2 e3 { Key: The king is driven to the back rank; 30.Kd2 Qxh1 wins a rook (engine -5.7). } 26. Bxe3 Bf5+ 27. Rd3 Qc4+ 28. Kd2 Qa2+ 29. Kd1 Qb1+ 0-1

[Event "7th DSB Congress, Dresden"]
[Site "Dresden"]
[Date "1892"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Siegbert Tarrasch"]
[Black "Georg Marco"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C62"]
[Themes "opening-trap,calculation,classic"]
[Level "1"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez: Berlin Defense, Tarrasch Trap"]
[Why "The 'Tarrasch Trap' in the Steinitz Defence of the Ruy Lopez: Black must exchange on d4 before castling. 7...O-O? loses a pawn by force (8.Bxc6 Bxc6 9.dxe5 dxe5 10.Qxd8 Raxd8 11.Nxe5), and Marco's attempt to win it back leaves a rook and a knight trapped after 16.Bg5! Rd5 17.Be7 Re8 18.c4. Eighteen moves, no sacrifice, pure opening knowledge; the trap caught several masters afterwards."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 d6 { Key: The Steinitz Defence: e5 is under pressure and 7...exd4 is required. } 4. d4 Bd7 5. Nc3 Nf6 6. O-O Be7 7. Re1 O-O { Key: A pawn falls because 11...Bxe4 runs into the pin on the e-file. } 8. Bxc6 Bxc6 9. dxe5 dxe5 10. Qxd8 Raxd8 11. Nxe5 Bxe4 { Key: The pawn is regained but Black's pieces are loose. } 12. Nxe4 Nxe4 13. Nd3 f5 14. f3 Bc5+ 15. Nxc5 Nxc5 16. Bg5 { Key: Rook d5 and knight c5 are both attacked; one is lost (engine +4.1). Marco resigned. } 16... Rd5 17. Be7 Re8 18. c4 1-0

[Event "Paris"]
[Site "Paris"]
[Date "1900"]
[Round "14"]
[White "Frank Marshall"]
[Black "Amos Burn"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "D55"]
[Themes "attack,pawn-storm,opening-trap"]
[Level "1"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Modern Variation, Normal Line"]
[Why "Seventeen moves that teach one pattern: after ...g6 the h-pawn runs to h5, the exchange on g6 opens the h-file, a bishop sacrifice on g6 removes the last pawn, and a rook check on h8 decoys the king into Qh7#. The anecdote (Burn was filling his pipe) matters less than the mechanism, which recurs in thousands of club games."]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. e3 O-O 6. Nf3 b6 7. Bd3 Bb7 8. cxd5 exd5 9. Bxf6 { Key: The h-pawn first; 10...g6? gives it a target (engine: 10...c5 keeps the balance). } 9... Bxf6 10. h4 g6 11. h5 { Key: The h-file is open; queen and bishop aim at g6. } 11... Re8 12. hxg6 hxg6 13. Qc2 Bg7 14. Bxg6 { Key: Every piece arrives with a threat (Qh7#, Qf7+). } 14... fxg6 15. Qxg6 Nd7 16. Ng5 Qf6 17. Rh8+ { Key: 17...Kxh8 18.Qh7# (mate in 1). } 1-0

[Event "Warsaw Olympiad"]
[Site "Warsaw"]
[Date "1935"]
[Round "14"]
[White "Paul Keres"]
[Black "William Winter"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "B29"]
[Themes "attack,sacrifice,development,opening-trap"]
[Level "2"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Nimzowitsch Variation, Advance Variation"]
[Why "A 19-move miniature by the 19-year-old Keres. He gives three pawns (9.Bd3!, 10.O-O, 11.Rb1) for a lead in development and crashes through on f7 and g6 (13.Nxf7!, 15.Bxg6+!). After 18.Rxe4! dxe4 19.Qf6+ the engine finds a forced mate. The arithmetic of development: three pawns do not help a king on e8 when every white piece is already in play."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. e5 Nd5 4. Nc3 e6 5. Nxd5 exd5 6. d4 d6 7. Bg5 { Key: Development instead of recapturing; after 9...dxc3 10.O-O cxb2 11.Rb1 White is three pawns down and fully mobilised. } 7... Qa5+ 8. c3 cxd4 9. Bd3 dxc3 10. O-O cxb2 11. Rb1 dxe5 12. Nxe5 { Key: Two pieces for the rook; the king is bare. } 12... Bd6 13. Nxf7 Kxf7 14. Qh5+ g6 15. Bxg6+ hxg6 16. Qxh8 Bf5 17. Rfe1 { Key: 19...Ke8 20.Qe6+ Kf8 21.Bh6#; 19...Kg8 20.Qxg6+ and mate follows (engine #11). Winter resigned. } 17... Be4 18. Rxe4 dxe4 19. Qf6+ 1-0

[Event "London (casual game, Simpson's Divan) - 'The Immortal Game'"]
[Site "London"]
[Date "1851"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Adolf Anderssen"]
[Black "Lionel Kieseritzky"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C33"]
[Themes "attack,sacrifice,immortal,classic"]
[Level "1"]
[Opening "King's Gambit Accepted: Bishop's Gambit, Bryan Countergambit"]
[Why "The most famous game ever played: White gives a bishop, both rooks and the queen to mate with three minor pieces while Black's queen and rook sit idle on a1 and h8. It is objectively flawed (engine: 18.Re1 instead of 18.Bd6 was +3.5, and 20...Ba6! would have resisted), which is itself a lesson: romantic attacks worked because defenders did not yet know how to return material. The timeless point is that time and activity beat material when the enemy king is stuck in the centre."]

1. e4 e5 2. f4 exf4 3. Bc4 Qh4+ 4. Kf1 b5 5. Bxb5 Nf6 6. Nf3 Qh6 7. d3 Nh5 8. Nh4 Qg5 9. Nf5 c6 10. g4 Nf6 11. Rg1 { Key: Anderssen lets the b5 bishop go (11...cxb5) to keep chasing the queen: 12.h4 Qg6 13.h5 Qg5 14.Qf3, every move with tempo. } 11... cxb5 12. h4 Qg6 13. h5 Qg5 14. Qf3 Ng8 15. Bxf4 Qf6 16. Nc3 Bc5 17. Nd5 { Key: The bishop blocks the c5 bishop's retreat and prepares Be7#; Black may take both rooks (18...Bxg1, 19...Qxa1+). Engine prefers 18.Re1, but 18.Bd6 is the move the game is remembered for. } 17... Qxb2 18. Bd6 Bxg1 19. e5 { Key: Cuts the queen on a1 off from g7 along the long diagonal. } 19... Qxa1+ 20. Ke2 { Key: 20...Ba6! was the defence (engine +2.0 for White, still fighting). After the game move it is mate. } 20... Na6 21. Nxg7+ { Key: The final queen sacrifice; mate with bishop and two knights. } 21... Kd8 22. Qf6+ Nxf6 23. Be7# 1-0

[Event "Berlin (casual game) - 'The Evergreen Game'"]
[Site "Berlin"]
[Date "1852"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Adolf Anderssen"]
[Black "Jean Dufresne"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C52"]
[Themes "attack,sacrifice,immortal,classic"]
[Level "2"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Evans Gambit, Dufresne Defense"]
[Why "The Evergreen: a model of the Evans Gambit attack (pawn for development, bishops on the a3-f8 and a2-g8 diagonals) finished by the quiet 19.Rad1!!, 20.Rxe7+! and 21.Qxd7+!!. Black's counterplay (19...Qxf3, threatening mate on g2) is ignored because White's forcing sequence comes first. Engine note: after 19.Rad1 Black could hold with 19...Qh3! (0.00), and 19.Be4 was objectively stronger, as Lasker argued; the combination is still the one to learn."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. b4 { Key: The Evans Gambit: two pawns for the centre and open lines. } 4... Bxb4 5. c3 Ba5 6. d4 exd4 7. O-O d3 8. Qb3 Qf6 9. e5 Qg6 10. Re1 Nge7 11. Ba3 { Key: The bishop on the a3-f8 diagonal keeps the king in the centre; 11...b5 12.Qxb5. } 11... b5 12. Qxb5 Rb8 13. Qa4 Bb6 14. Nbd2 Bb7 15. Ne4 Qf5 16. Bxd3 Qh5 17. Nf6+ { Key: The e-file and g-file open; Black counterattacks g2 with 19...Qxf3. } 17... gxf6 18. exf6 Rg8 19. Rad1 { Key: Rook and queen sacrificed; the two bishops mate. } 19... Qxf3 20. Rxe7+ Nxe7 21. Qxd7+ Kxd7 22. Bf5+ Ke8 23. Bd7+ Kf8 24. Bxe7# 1-0

[Event "London (international tournament)"]
[Site "London"]
[Date "1883"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Johannes Zukertort"]
[Black "Joseph Blackburne"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "A13"]
[Themes "attack,sacrifice,calculation,classic"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "English Opening: Agincourt Defense"]
[Why "The most admired attacking game of the 19th century. Zukertort builds a pawn roller e4-e5-f4-f5, lifts a rook (21.Re3), and when Blackburne's rooks invade the second rank he ignores them and plays 28.Qb4!!, a queen offer that cannot be accepted (28...Qxb4 29.Bxe5+ and forced mate, engine #7). In a race of attacks, count tempi; a quiet non-checking queen move can be the strongest move on the board."]

1. c4 e6 2. e3 Nf6 3. Nf3 b6 4. Be2 Bb7 5. O-O d5 6. d4 Bd6 7. Nc3 O-O 8. b3 Nbd7 9. Bb2 Qe7 10. Nb5 { Key: White takes the bishop pair and leaves Black a slightly weak d-pawn. } 10... Ne4 11. Nxd6 cxd6 12. Nd2 Ndf6 13. f3 Nxd2 14. Qxd2 dxc4 15. Bxc4 d5 16. Bd3 Rfc8 17. Rae1 Rc7 18. e4 { Key: The pawn storm and the rook lift; Black's pieces are on the queenside. } 18... Rac8 19. e5 Ne8 20. f4 g6 21. Re3 f5 22. exf6 Nxf6 23. f5 Ne4 24. Bxe4 dxe4 25. fxg6 { Key: Black's rooks reach the second rank, but the discovered check comes first. } 25... Rc2 26. gxh7+ Kh8 27. d5+ e5 28. Qb4 { Key: Deflection: 28...Qxb4 29.Bxe5+ Kxh7 30.Rh3+ Kg6 31.Rf6+ Kg7 32.Rg3+ and mate (engine #7). } 28... R8c5 { Key: Material and mate threats; engine #10. Blackburne resigned. } 29. Rf8+ Kxh7 30. Qxe4+ Kg7 31. Bxe5+ Kxf8 32. Bg7+ Kg8 33. Qxe7 1-0

[Event "Hastings"]
[Site "Hastings"]
[Date "1895"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Wilhelm Steinitz"]
[Black "Curt von Bardeleben"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C54"]
[Themes "attack,calculation,development,classic"]
[Level "2"]
[Opening "Italian Game: Giuoco Piano, Greco's Attack"]
[Why "The model attack on a king that never castles. Steinitz opens the centre with 17.d5!, plants a knight on e6 and finishes with 22.Rxe7+!!, a rook that cannot be taken (22...Kxe7 23.Re1+; 22...Qxe7 23.Rxc8+; engine +5 either way). Bardeleben left the hall rather than resign, and Steinitz demonstrated the mate in ten to the spectators; the announced line is a classic exercise in queen checks along the seventh and eighth ranks."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. c3 Nf6 5. d4 exd4 6. cxd4 Bb4+ 7. Nc3 d5 8. exd5 Nxd5 9. O-O { Key: Exchanges that leave Black's king on e8 with the e-file open. } 9... Be6 10. Bg5 Be7 11. Bxd5 Bxd5 12. Nxd5 Qxd5 13. Bxe7 Nxe7 14. Re1 f6 15. Qe2 Qd7 16. Rac1 { Key: The knight on e6 fixes the king and the c8 rook. } 16... c6 17. d5 cxd5 18. Nd4 Kf7 19. Ne6 Rhc8 20. Qg4 { Key: Steinitz's line: 22...Kxe7 23.Re1+ Kd6 24.Qb4+ Kc7 25.Ne6+ Kb8 26.Qf4+ and wins; 22...Qxe7 23.Rxc8+ Rxc8 24.Qxc8+ with a winning ending. } 20... g6 21. Ng5+ Ke8 22. Rxe7+ Kf8 { Key: The rook is immune on every square because of Rxc8+ and Qxd7. } 23. Rf7+ Kg8 24. Rg7+ Kh8 25. Rxh7+ 1-0

[Event "Amsterdam"]
[Site "Amsterdam"]
[Date "1889"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Emanuel Lasker"]
[Black "Johann Bauer"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "A03"]
[Themes "attack,sacrifice,classic"]
[Level "2"]
[Opening "Bird Opening: Dutch Variation"]
[Why "The original double bishop sacrifice. 15.Bxh7+! and 17.Bxg7! strip the king, 19.Rf3-h3 brings the rook with mate threats, and the point of the whole operation is the quiet 22.Qd7!, a double attack on the two black bishops that regains a piece and leaves White a clear pawn up (engine +4.8). Preconditions to teach: two bishops aimed at h7 and g7, a queen that can reach h5, a rook lift, and no black knight on f6. Nimzowitsch-Tarrasch 1914 and many later games copy the pattern."]

1. f4 d5 2. e3 Nf6 3. b3 e6 4. Bb2 Be7 5. Bd3 b6 6. Nf3 Bb7 7. Nc3 Nbd7 8. O-O O-O 9. Ne2 c5 10. Ng3 Qc7 11. Ne5 Nxe5 12. Bxe5 Qc6 13. Qe2 a6 14. Nh5 { Key: Deflects the f6 knight, the defender of h7 (engine +1.7 before, +4.8 after the sacrifice is accepted). } 14... Nxh5 15. Bxh7+ { Key: Both bishops given; the rook lift threatens Rh3 mate. } 15... Kxh7 16. Qxh5+ Kg8 17. Bxg7 Kxg7 18. Qg4+ Kh7 19. Rf3 e5 { Key: The double attack on b7 and e7 is what makes the sacrifice sound. } 20. Rh3+ Qh6 21. Rxh6+ Kxh6 22. Qd7 Bf6 23. Qxb7 Kg7 24. Rf1 Rab8 25. Qd7 Rfd8 26. Qg4+ Kf8 27. fxe5 Bg7 28. e6 Rb7 29. Qg6 f6 30. Rxf6+ { Key: Liquidation into a won ending; 38.Qxd3 and Bauer resigned. } 30... Bxf6 31. Qxf6+ Ke8 32. Qh8+ Ke7 33. Qg7+ Kxe6 34. Qxb7 Rd6 35. Qxa6 d4 36. exd4 cxd4 37. h4 d3 38. Qxd3 1-0

[Event "World Championship match, Havana, game 4"]
[Site "Havana"]
[Date "1892"]
[Round "4"]
[White "Wilhelm Steinitz"]
[Black "Mikhail Chigorin"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C65"]
[Themes "attack,bishop-pair,sacrifice,world-championship"]
[Level "2"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez: Berlin Defense, Anti-Berlin Variation"]
[Why "Steinitz's slow Ruy Lopez regrouping (d3, c3, Nbd2-f1-e3, bishop to b3) prepares the h-pawn attack against a fianchettoed king. After 21.d4! both bishops' diagonals open at once and 24.Rxh7+!! Kxh7 25.Qh1+ wins by force (engine: mate in 6 from move 24). Teaching points: the rook's pawn against a fianchetto, two bishops aimed at the king, and the double rook sacrifice that converts them."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. d3 { Key: The Steinitz regrouping: knight to e3, bishop kept on the a2-g8 diagonal. } 4... d6 5. c3 g6 6. Nbd2 Bg7 7. Nf1 O-O 8. Ba4 Nd7 9. Ne3 Nc5 10. Bc2 Ne6 11. h4 { Key: The h-file opens; 16.Bb3 and 18.Be3 point both bishops at the king. } 11... Ne7 12. h5 d5 13. hxg6 fxg6 14. exd5 Nxd5 15. Nxd5 Qxd5 16. Bb3 Qc6 17. Qe2 Bd7 18. Be3 Kh8 19. O-O-O { Key: A rook is given to open the long diagonal and the h-file together. } 19... Rae8 20. Qf1 a5 21. d4 exd4 22. Nxd4 Bxd4 23. Rxd4 Nxd4 24. Rxh7+ { Key: 28...Kf5 29.g4# (engine #1). Chigorin resigned. } 24... Kxh7 25. Qh1+ Kg7 26. Bh6+ Kf6 27. Qh4+ Ke5 28. Qxd4+ 1-0

[Event "Hastings"]
[Site "Hastings"]
[Date "1895"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Harry Nelson Pillsbury"]
[Black "Siegbert Tarrasch"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "D55"]
[Themes "attack,pawn-storm,pawn-structure,classic"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Orthodox Defense, Pillsbury Variation"]
[Why "The classic race between a queenside pawn majority (Tarrasch: ...c4, ...b5-b4, ...c3) and a kingside attack (Pillsbury: Ne5, f4, Qf3-g3-h4, g4-g5). Tarrasch's passed pawn reaches c2 but White mates first. The engine shows how close it was: 28...c3! (instead of 28...Qa4) and 35...h6 would have favoured Black (about -2). The lesson is the Pillsbury formation (Bg5, Ne5, f4, Qf3, rook lift) and the rule that a wing attack beats a queenside majority only if every move carries a threat."]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. Nf3 Nbd7 6. Rc1 O-O 7. e3 b6 8. cxd5 exd5 9. Bd3 Bb7 10. O-O c5 11. Re1 c4 12. Bb1 a6 13. Ne5 { Key: The Pillsbury set-up; the queen goes to the kingside with tempo. } 13... b5 14. f4 Re8 15. Qf3 Nf8 16. Ne2 Ne4 17. Bxe7 Rxe7 18. Bxe4 dxe4 19. Qg3 f6 20. Ng4 Kh8 21. f5 { Key: Rook and queen lift; Black's pieces are far away. } 21... Qd7 22. Rf1 Rd8 23. Rf4 Qd6 24. Qh4 Rde8 25. Nc3 Bd5 26. Nf2 Qc6 27. Rf1 b4 { Key: 28...c3! was stronger (engine -2.0); the majority advances one move too slowly. } 28. Ne2 Qa4 29. Ng4 Nd7 30. R4f2 Kg8 31. Nc1 c3 32. b3 Qc6 33. h3 a5 34. Nh2 a4 35. g4 { Key: White gives material to open the g-file; 42...Rg7 43.Rxg7 Kxg7 44.Qg3+ Kxh6. } 35... axb3 36. axb3 Ra8 37. g5 Ra3 38. Ng4 Bxb3 39. Rg2 Kh8 40. gxf6 gxf6 41. Nxb3 Rxb3 42. Nh6 Rg7 43. Rxg7 Kxg7 44. Qg3+ Kxh6 45. Kh1 { Key: A quiet king move renews the threat Rg1; the c-pawn is one move from queening when mate arrives (engine +4.0 after 45.Kh1). } 45... Qd5 46. Rg1 Qxf5 47. Qh4+ Qh5 48. Qf4+ Qg5 49. Rxg5 fxg5 50. Qd6+ Kh5 51. Qxd7 c2 52. Qxh7# 1-0

[Event "Lodz - 'Rubinstein's Immortal'"]
[Site "Lodz"]
[Date "1907"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Georg Rotlewi"]
[Black "Akiba Rubinstein"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "D40"]
[Themes "attack,sacrifice,calculation,immortal"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "Tarrasch Defense: Symmetrical Variation"]
[Why "Both sides reach mirror-image set-ups, but White wastes two tempi with the bishop (11.Bd3 dxc4 12.Bxc4 b5 13.Bd3), so Black is a move ahead when the position opens. Rubinstein then gives queen and both rooks: 22...Rxc3!! 23.gxh4 Rd2!! 24.Qxd2 Bxe4+ 25.Qg2 Rh3! and mate on h2 cannot be stopped (engine: mate in 3 at best for White). When all your pieces point at the king and his do not, material is irrelevant; also the power of two bishops on open diagonals."]

1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 e6 3. e3 c5 4. c4 Nc6 5. Nc3 Nf6 6. dxc5 Bxc5 7. a3 a6 8. b4 Bd6 9. Bb2 O-O 10. Qd2 { Key: White loses two tempi; Black's identical set-up is a move ahead. } 10... Qe7 11. Bd3 dxc4 12. Bxc4 b5 13. Bd3 Rd8 14. Qe2 Bb7 15. O-O Ne5 { Key: White's central advance only opens the diagonals a8-h1 and a7-g1 for Black's bishops. } 16. Nxe5 Bxe5 17. f4 Bc7 18. e4 Rac8 19. e5 Bb6+ 20. Kh1 Ng4 21. Be4 Qh4 22. g3 Rxc3 { Key: 22...Rxc3!! The rook is immune: 23.Bxc3 Bxe4+ 24.Qxe4 Qxh2# is mate. } 23. gxh4 { Key: Queen and second rook given; 26.Qxe4 Rxh2# or 26.Rf2 Bxf2 27.Qxe4 Rxh2#. Rotlewi resigned. } 23... Rd2 24. Qxd2 Bxe4+ 25. Qg2 Rh3 0-1

[Event "San Sebastian (round 1)"]
[Site "San Sebastian"]
[Date "1911"]
[Round "1"]
[White "Jose Raul Capablanca"]
[Black "Ossip Bernstein"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C66"]
[Themes "attack,sacrifice,calculation,initiative"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez: Berlin Defense"]
[Why "Capablanca's first-round win at his first international tournament, awarded the brilliancy prize. He lets Bernstein's queen take two pawns on the queenside (22...Qxa2, 23...Qxc2) while every white piece gathers on the kingside, then 28.Nfxg7! breaks the king open; the engine confirms the attack (+1.9 after 28.Nfxg7) and 33.f5+ Kg5 34.Qe3+ leads to mate in 6. A queen far from its king is as bad as a missing piece."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 Nf6 4. O-O Be7 5. Nc3 d6 6. Bxc6+ { Key: Black has doubled c-pawns; White's pieces all point at the kingside. } 6... bxc6 7. d4 exd4 8. Nxd4 Bd7 9. Bg5 O-O 10. Re1 h6 11. Bh4 Nh7 12. Bxe7 Qxe7 13. Qd3 Rab8 14. b3 Ng5 15. Rad1 Qe5 16. Qe3 Ne6 17. Nce2 { Key: Knights on f5 and d4, then the g-pawn advances. } 17... Qa5 18. Nf5 Nc5 19. Ned4 Kh7 20. g4 Rbe8 21. f3 { Key: Black's queen has taken two pawns and is three moves from the defence. } 21... Ne6 22. Ne2 Qxa2 23. Neg3 Qxc2 24. Rc1 Qb2 25. Nh5 Rh8 26. Re2 Qe5 27. f4 Qb5 28. Nfxg7 { Key: The king is driven into the open; mate in 6 (34...Kxg4 35.Nf6+ or 34...Kh4 35.Qg3+). Bernstein resigned. } 28... Nc5 29. Nxe8 Bxe8 30. Qc3 f6 31. Nxf6+ Kg6 32. Nh5 Rg8 33. f5+ Kg5 34. Qe3+ 1-0

[Event "Moscow (international tournament)"]
[Site "Moscow"]
[Date "1925"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Jose Raul Capablanca"]
[Black "Efim Bogoljubov"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "D24"]
[Themes "attack,sacrifice,calculation"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted: Rosenthal Variation"]
[Why "A king hunt that continues after the queens are gone. Capablanca's 9.Bxe6! fxe6 10.Nxe6 drags the king out (engine +1.1, so this is a practical rather than a forced win), and after 17.Qxb6 the king on h5 is still hunted by rooks and knights until 32.Re7+ wins a piece. The lesson is that king safety is not only a problem with queens on the board, and that rooks and knights coordinate well against an exposed king."]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 dxc4 4. e4 c5 5. Bxc4 cxd4 6. Nxd4 Nf6 7. Nc3 Bc5 { Key: A bishop for two pawns and a king that can no longer castle. } 8. Be3 Nbd7 9. Bxe6 fxe6 10. Nxe6 Qa5 11. O-O { Key: The rook on f5 and the knight on e6 form a net: 14...Qb6 15.Nf4+ Kh6 16.g4. } 11... Bxe3 12. fxe3 Kf7 13. Qb3 Kg6 14. Rf5 Qb6 15. Nf4+ Kh6 16. g4 g5 17. Qxb6 { Key: Queens are exchanged and the hunt goes on with rooks and knights. } 17... axb6 18. Rd1 Rg8 19. Nfd5 Nxg4 20. Ne7 Rg7 21. Rd6+ { Key: The king shuttles between g4 and h5; 25...Rg6 26.Ne7. } 21... Kh5 22. Rf3 Ngf6 23. Rh3+ Kg4 24. Rg3+ Kh5 25. Nf5 Rg6 26. Ne7 g4 27. Nxg6 Kxg6 28. Rxg4+ Kf7 29. Rf4 Kg7 30. e5 Ne8 31. Re6 Nc7 32. Re7+ { Key: Bogoljubov resigned. After 32...Kg6 33.e6 Nc5 34.Rxc7 White wins a piece (engine +4.7). } 1-0

[Event "World Championship Tournament, Moscow (round 10)"]
[Site "Moscow"]
[Date "1948"]
[Round "10"]
[White "Mikhail Botvinnik"]
[Black "Paul Keres"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "E28"]
[Themes "attack,exchange-sacrifice,pawn-structure,world-championship"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "Nimzo-Indian Defense: Sämisch Variation"]
[Why "The game that put Botvinnik on course for the title. Keres plays the Nimzo-Indian passively (6...Re8, 12...Nb6?!), lets White build the full centre with 14.e4, and is crushed by a rook lift along the fifth rank (18.Rxc5, 20.Rg5!) and the exchange sacrifice 21.Rxg7+! (engine: mate in 10 after 23.Qe3). A model for using the Saemisch pawn centre and the g-file once Black's knights leave the kingside."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 O-O 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 Re8 7. Ne2 { Key: White keeps the bishops and the c3/c4/d4 centre; 11...cxd4 12.cxd4 Nb6?! takes a defender away from the king. } 7... e5 8. Ng3 d6 9. Be2 Nbd7 10. O-O c5 11. f3 cxd4 12. cxd4 Nb6 13. Bb2 exd4 14. e4 { Key: The c-pawn opens the c-file and the rook joins the attack from the side. } 14... Be6 15. Rc1 Re7 16. Qxd4 Qc7 17. c5 dxc5 18. Rxc5 Qf4 { Key: The rook swings to the g-file: Rxg7+ is threatened. } 19. Bc1 Qb8 20. Rg5 Nbd7 { Key: 23...Nxh5 24.Qh6#; otherwise Qg5+/Qh6 (engine #10). Keres resigned. } 21. Rxg7+ Kxg7 22. Nh5+ Kg6 23. Qe3 1-0

[Event "Candidates Tournament, Budapest (round 18)"]
[Site "Budapest"]
[Date "1950"]
[Round "18"]
[White "David Bronstein"]
[Black "Paul Keres"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C91"]
[Themes "attack,pawn-storm,sacrifice,initiative"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez: Closed, Bogoljubow Variation"]
[Why "Bronstein gives a pawn for the centre and the bishop pair (12.Qd1! dxc3 13.Nxc3; engine about -0.4, so the compensation is real but not more), then launches f4-f5-f6 with queen and bishop against the dark squares around g7. After 27.Bxg7 Kxg7 28.f6+ Kh8 29.Qg5 the king is in a mating net and 33.Qh6 (threat Qg7#) is mate in 4. The pawn-sacrifice-for-initiative style that won Bronstein the Candidates."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. d4 d6 9. c3 Bg4 { Key: Bronstein does not recapture; the pawn buys a big centre and two bishops. } 10. h3 Bxf3 11. Qxf3 exd4 12. Qd1 dxc3 13. Nxc3 Na5 14. Bc2 Re8 15. f4 { Key: The knight exchange opens the a2-g8 diagonal and prepares f5. } 15... b4 16. Nd5 Nxd5 17. Qxd5 c6 18. Qd3 g6 19. Kh1 Bf8 20. Rf1 Bg7 21. Bd2 c5 22. Ba4 Rf8 23. Rab1 Qb6 24. f5 { Key: The dark squares g7 and f6 are the target: 26...Bg7 27.Bxg7 Kxg7 28.f6+ Kh8 29.Qg5. } 24... Bd4 25. Qg3 Nc4 26. Bh6 Bg7 27. Bxg7 Kxg7 28. f6+ Kh8 29. Qg5 b3 { Key: Black's queenside counterplay is too slow; Qg7# cannot be stopped (engine #4). Keres resigned. } 30. axb3 Qb4 31. bxc4 Qxa4 32. Rf4 Qc2 33. Qh6 1-0

[Event "Prague-Moscow match, Moscow (board game 6)"]
[Site "Moscow"]
[Date "1946"]
[Round "6"]
[White "Frantisek Zita"]
[Black "David Bronstein"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "E68"]
[Themes "attack,exchange-sacrifice,initiative,calculation"]
[Level "4"]
[Opening "King's Indian Defense: Fianchetto Variation, Double Fianchetto Attack"]
[Why "An early King's Indian masterpiece that shows the defence's dynamic idea. Black gives the exchange (17...Rxa1 18.Rxa1 Nxf2!, engine -2.8) to rip open the king position and collects pawns with knight forks; every black piece then joins the attack while White's queenside rooks watch. In the King's Indian the 'weak' d6 pawn does not matter when Black's pieces reach f2, e4 and g4; the exchange is cheap when it buys the initiative."]

1. c4 e5 2. Nc3 Nf6 3. Nf3 d6 4. d4 Nbd7 5. g3 g6 6. Bg2 Bg7 7. O-O O-O 8. b3 Re8 { Key: Pressure on d4 and f2 along the a7-g1 diagonal. } 9. Bb2 c6 10. e4 exd4 11. Nxd4 Qb6 12. Qd2 { Key: The exchange for two pawns and the king's cover. } 12... Nc5 13. Rfe1 a5 14. Rab1 a4 15. Ba1 axb3 16. axb3 Ng4 17. h3 Rxa1 18. Rxa1 Nxf2 19. Re3 { Key: Three pawns for the exchange and every black piece in the attack (engine -2.8). } 19... Nxh3+ 20. Kh2 Nf2 21. Rf3 Ncxe4 22. Qf4 Ng4+ 23. Kh1 f5 24. Nxe4 { Key: The queen swings to h6 against h2; engine: mate in 14. Zita resigned. } 24... Rxe4 25. Qxd6 Rxd4 26. Qb8 Rd8 27. Ra8 Be5 28. Qa7 Qb4 29. Qa2 Qf8 30. Bh3 Qh6 0-1

[Event "St Petersburg (final group)"]
[Site "St Petersburg"]
[Date "1914"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Emanuel Lasker"]
[Black "Jose Raul Capablanca"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C68"]
[Themes "positional,initiative,endgame,classic"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez: Exchange Variation, Alekhine Variation"]
[Why "Lasker, needing a win against the tournament leader, chooses the quiet Exchange Variation and an early queen trade, then plays the queenless position for activity: 12.f5! fixes e6 and shuts in the c8 bishop (objectively doubtful, engine prefers Black after 12...c5!, but Capablanca answered passively), 14.Bxd6! and 16.Ne6! plant the famous knight, and the white king marches up the board (23.g4, 27.Kf3, 28.Kf4) before 35.e5! breaks through. A 'drawish' structure can be played for a win with a plan and active pieces, and a passive opponent loses without a single tactic."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Bxc6 { Key: Exchange Variation with queens off: White's 4-v-3 kingside majority against Black's bishop pair. } 4... dxc6 5. d4 exd4 6. Qxd4 Qxd4 7. Nxd4 Bd6 8. Nc3 Ne7 9. O-O O-O 10. f4 Re8 11. Nb3 f6 { Key: The pawn on f5 takes e6 from Black's pieces and buries the c8 bishop. Engine: 12...c5! would have given Black the better game; Capablanca played 12...b6. } 12. f5 b6 13. Bf4 Bb7 14. Bxd6 { Key: Bishop for knight to install the knight on e6, which nothing can challenge. } 14... cxd6 15. Nd4 Rad8 16. Ne6 Rd7 17. Rad1 Nc8 { Key: 17...c5! was the last chance (engine +0.8 after 18...b5); the queenside is fixed. 23.g4, 27.Kf3, 28.Kf4: the king becomes an attacking piece. } 18. Rf2 b5 19. Rfd2 Rde7 20. b4 Kf7 21. a3 Ba8 22. Kf2 Ra7 23. g4 h6 24. Rd3 a5 25. h4 axb4 26. axb4 Rae7 27. Kf3 Rg8 28. Kf4 g6 29. Rg3 g5+ 30. Kf3 Nb6 31. hxg5 hxg5 32. Rh3 Rd7 33. Kg3 Ke8 34. Rdh1 Bb7 35. e5 { Key: Both rooks invade; Capablanca resigned. } 35... dxe5 36. Ne4 Nd5 37. N6c5 Bc8 38. Nxd7 Bxd7 39. Rh7 Rf8 40. Ra1 Kd8 41. Ra8+ Bc8 42. Nc5 1-0

[Event "Baltimore (match game)"]
[Site "Baltimore"]
[Date "1885"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Wilhelm Steinitz"]
[Black "Alexander Sellman"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C11"]
[Themes "positional,pawn-structure,blockade,knight-vs-bishop"]
[Level "2"]
[Opening "French Defense: Classical Variation, Steinitz Variation"]
[Why "The earliest famous demonstration of playing against a 'bad bishop'. The French pawn chain leaves Black's light-squared bishop buried on a8 behind d5 and e6; Steinitz exchanges the pieces that could free it, wins the c-file (24.Rxc8 Qxc8 25.Rc1) and brings a knight on the long route Nd1-c3-b1-d2-b3-a5-c6 to invade. After 35.Bd6 every black piece is tied up and the a8 bishop has never moved. One bad piece can decide a game."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 { Key: The e5 wedge; the c8 bishop will be a prisoner behind its own pawns. } 4... Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. dxc5 Bxc5 7. Nf3 a6 8. Bd3 Nc6 9. Qe2 Nb4 10. Bd2 b5 11. Nd1 { Key: Steinitz accepts doubled d-pawns for the c-file and control of c5. } 11... Nxd3+ 12. cxd3 Qb6 13. b4 Be7 14. a3 f5 15. Rc1 Bb7 16. Be3 Qd8 17. Nd4 Nf8 18. O-O h5 19. Nc3 { Key: The knight travels to a5 to hit c6 and b7; the bishop retreats to a8 for good. } 19... Kf7 20. Nb1 g6 21. Nd2 Nd7 22. N2b3 Rc8 23. Na5 Ba8 24. Rxc8 { Key: The c-file and the c6 square belong to White (engine +4.7). } 24... Qxc8 25. Rc1 Qb8 26. Qc2 Bd8 27. Nac6 Qb7 28. Nxd8+ Rxd8 29. Qc7 { Key: Complete paralysis (engine +5.7). Sellman resigned. } 29... Qb8 30. Bf2 Qb6 31. Nf3 Qxc7 32. Rxc7 Ke8 33. Ng5 Nf8 34. Bc5 Nd7 35. Bd6 1-0

[Event "New York (round 3)"]
[Site "New York"]
[Date "1927"]
[Round "3"]
[White "Aron Nimzowitsch"]
[Black "Jose Raul Capablanca"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "B12"]
[Themes "positional,pawn-structure,endgame,classic"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "Caro-Kann Defense: Advance Variation"]
[Why "Capablanca's answer to the Advance Caro-Kann: trade the light-squared bishops (3...Bf5, 4...Bxd3), exchange the dark bishop with ...c5 and ...Bxc5, then play against the dark-square holes and White's over-extended pawns on e5, f4 and g5. The doubled rooks on the c-file, 36...Rcxd4! and the invasion 41...Qc1 and 42...Qh1 show how a heavy-piece position is won by getting behind the pawns. Engine: equal after 13.f4, -12 at the end; the game is a chain of small positional decisions, not one blunder."]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 Bf5 { Key: Both black bishops are traded or active; White's dark squares d4 and f4 are weak. } 4. Bd3 Bxd3 5. Qxd3 e6 6. Nc3 Qb6 7. Nge2 c5 8. dxc5 Bxc5 9. O-O Ne7 10. Na4 Qc6 11. Nxc5 Qxc5 12. Be3 Qc7 13. f4 { Key: White's kingside pawns are fixed on dark squares; the e3 bishop is gone. } 13... Nf5 14. c3 Nc6 15. Rad1 g6 16. g4 Nxe3 17. Qxe3 h5 18. g5 O-O 19. Nd4 { Key: Rooks doubled on the c-file, pressure on d4 and f4. } 19... Qb6 20. Rf2 Rfc8 21. a3 Rc7 22. Rd3 Na5 23. Re2 Re8 24. Kg2 Nc6 25. Red2 Rec8 26. Re2 Ne7 27. Red2 Rc4 28. Qh3 Kg7 29. Rf2 a5 30. Re2 Nf5 31. Nxf5+ gxf5 32. Qf3 Kg6 33. Red2 Re4 34. Rd4 Rc4 35. Qf2 Qb5 36. Kg3 Rcxd4 { Key: Rooks exchanged; now b2, d4 and f4 are all targets for the queen. } 37. cxd4 Qc4 38. Kg2 b5 39. Kg1 b4 40. axb4 axb4 41. Kg2 Qc1 { Key: Queen and rook get behind White's lines; f4 falls (engine -12). Nimzowitsch resigned. } 42. Kg3 Qh1 43. Rd3 Re1 44. Rf3 Rd1 45. b3 Rc1 46. Re3 Rf1 0-1

[Event "World Championship match, Buenos Aires, game 21"]
[Site "Buenos Aires"]
[Date "1927"]
[Round "21"]
[White "Jose Raul Capablanca"]
[Black "Alexander Alekhine"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "D63"]
[Themes "positional,initiative,calculation,world-championship"]
[Level "4"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Orthodox Defense, Henneberger Variation"]
[Why "Alekhine's most harmonious game of the match: the freeing plan of the Orthodox QGD (...a6, ...dxc4, ...b5, ...Bb7, ...c5) completed, knights to c4 and d5, a rook to c4 and the queen behind the bishop on the long diagonal (21...Qa8!). Capablanca's 15.b4?! and 20.Rxc4 leave him without counterplay and 32...Nxe3! wins at once (engine -5.6). Coordinated pieces on open files and diagonals beat a solid but passive set-up; the win is positional even though it ends with a tactic."]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Nbd7 5. e3 Be7 6. Nf3 O-O 7. Rc1 a6 { Key: The freeing plan completed; the b7 bishop eyes the long diagonal. } 8. a3 h6 9. Bh4 dxc4 10. Bxc4 b5 11. Be2 Bb7 12. O-O c5 13. dxc5 Nxc5 14. Nd4 Rc8 15. b4 { Key: The knights take c4 and d5; the rook on c4 cannot be challenged. } 15... Ncd7 16. Bg3 Nb6 17. Qb3 Nfd5 18. Bf3 Rc4 19. Ne4 Qc8 20. Rxc4 { Key: The queen behind the bishop; after 24.Bxd5 Qxd5 Black's queen and knight dominate. } 20... Nxc4 21. Rc1 Qa8 22. Nc3 Rc8 23. Nxd5 Bxd5 24. Bxd5 Qxd5 25. a4 { Key: 33.fxe3 Bxe3+ or 33.Rxd4 Qxd4 wins. Capablanca resigned. } 25... Bf6 26. Nf3 Bb2 27. Re1 Rd8 28. axb5 axb5 29. h3 e5 30. Rb1 e4 31. Nd4 Bxd4 32. Rd1 Nxe3 0-1

[Event "San Remo - 'Alekhine's Gun'"]
[Site "San Remo"]
[Date "1930"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Alexander Alekhine"]
[Black "Aron Nimzowitsch"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C17"]
[Themes "positional,zugzwang,initiative,classic"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "French Defense: Winawer Variation, Bogoljubow Variation"]
[Why "The queen behind two rooks on the c-file (24.Rc3, 25.R1c2, 26.Qc1) against the pinned c6 knight: all of Black's pieces are tied to c6 and c7, so after 30.h4 every move loses material (zugzwang with queens on; engine +5). Also a model of the Winawer with 5.Bd2 and 6.Nb5!, and of the a-pawn march a4-a5-a6 that fixes the queenside. One weak point attacked by every piece wins without a sacrifice."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. Bd2 { Key: White prevents ...Bxb5 and starts the a-pawn. } 5... Ne7 6. Nb5 Bxd2+ 7. Qxd2 O-O 8. c3 b6 9. f4 Ba6 10. Nf3 Qd7 11. a4 Nbc6 12. b4 { Key: The pawn on a6 and the bishop on b5 make c6 a permanent target. } 12... cxb4 13. cxb4 Bb7 14. Nd6 f5 15. a5 Nc8 16. Nxb7 Qxb7 17. a6 Qf7 18. Bb5 N8e7 19. O-O h6 20. Rfc1 { Key: The gun is loaded: Rc3, Rc2, Qc1 against c6. } 20... Rfc8 21. Rc2 Qe8 22. Rac1 Rab8 23. Qe3 Rc7 24. Rc3 Qd7 25. R1c2 Kf8 26. Qc1 Rbc8 { Key: Zugzwang: any move drops c6 or allows b5 (engine +5.1). Many sources end here; this source continues 30...h5 31.Kh2 g6 32.g3 1-0. } 27. Ba4 b5 28. Bxb5 Ke8 29. Ba4 Kd8 30. h4 h5 31. Kh2 g6 32. g3 1-0

[Event "Karlsbad"]
[Site "Karlsbad"]
[Date "1911"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Aron Nimzowitsch"]
[Black "Georg Salwe"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C02"]
[Themes "blockade,positional,prophylaxis,pawn-structure"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "French Defense: Advance Variation, Milner-Barry Gambit"]
[Why "The game Nimzowitsch used in My System to explain the blockade. Instead of defending the base of the pawn chain he gives up d4 (7.dxc5!) and blockades d4 and e5 with pieces (Bd4, Ne5), overprotecting them so they cannot be driven off; then the blockaders become attackers (23.Rg3, 24.Bxh7!). A blockading piece on a central square is stronger than the pawn it replaces, and overprotection produces flexible, strong pieces."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. e5 c5 4. c3 Nc6 5. Nf3 Qb6 { Key: White gives the d4 pawn away and gains time on the bishop. } 6. Bd3 Bd7 7. dxc5 Bxc5 8. O-O f6 9. b4 Be7 10. Bf4 { Key: The e5 square is held by pieces (Be5, Nf3), not by the pawn. } 10... fxe5 11. Nxe5 Nxe5 12. Bxe5 Nf6 13. Nd2 O-O 14. Nf3 Bd6 15. Qe2 Rac8 16. Bd4 { Key: The pieces rotate through d4 and e5; the e6 pawn and e8 bishop are shut in. } 16... Qc7 17. Ne5 Be8 18. Rae1 Bxe5 19. Bxe5 Qc6 20. Bd4 Bd7 21. Qc2 { Key: The blockade turns into a kingside attack (engine +3.0 after 24.Bxh7). } 21... Rf7 22. Re3 b6 23. Rg3 Kh8 24. Bxh7 e5 25. Bg6 Re7 26. Re1 Qd6 27. Be3 d4 28. Bg5 { Key: Salwe's exchange sacrifice changes nothing; 39.h4 and Black resigned. } 28... Rxc3 29. Rxc3 dxc3 30. Qxc3 Kg8 31. a3 Kf8 32. Bh4 Be8 33. Bf5 Qd4 34. Qxd4 exd4 35. Rxe7 Kxe7 36. Bd3 Kd6 37. Bxf6 gxf6 38. Kf1 Bc6 39. h4 1-0

[Event "Dresden (round 2)"]
[Site "Dresden"]
[Date "1926"]
[Round "2"]
[White "Paul Johner"]
[Black "Aron Nimzowitsch"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "E50"]
[Themes "prophylaxis,positional,blockade,pawn-structure"]
[Level "4"]
[Opening "Nimzo-Indian Defense: Rubinstein System, Hübner Variation"]
[Why "Nimzowitsch's 'immortal game of prophylaxis'. Black fixes the doubled c-pawns (7...Bxc3, 8...d6), closes the centre with 11...e4!, and then plays the famous queen tour ...Qd7-f5-h7 (14...h5!, 15...Qf5!, 16...Qh7!!) whose only purpose is to stop White's freeing breaks g2-g4 and f4-f5. Only after White is immobilised does the attack begin (24...g5!). The model game for 'restrain, blockade, destroy' and for moves that prevent the opponent's plan instead of advancing one's own."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 O-O 5. Bd3 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. O-O Bxc3 { Key: The centre is closed, c3/c4 are fixed targets and e4 becomes an outpost. } 8. bxc3 d6 9. Nd2 b6 10. Nb3 e5 11. f4 e4 12. Be2 { Key: The queen on h7 and pawn on h5 stop g4 and f5; White has no plan (engine -1.3). } 12... Qd7 13. h3 Ne7 14. Qe1 h5 15. Bd2 Qf5 16. Kh2 Qh7 17. a4 { Key: ...a5 fixes a4 and c4; Black's pieces occupy the blockading squares. } 17... Nf5 18. g3 a5 19. Rg1 Nh6 20. Bf1 Bd7 21. Bc1 Rac8 22. d5 Kh8 23. Nd2 Rg8 24. Bg2 { Key: After restraint, the attack on the g-file. } 24... g5 25. Nf1 Rg7 26. Ra2 Nf5 27. Bh1 Rcg8 28. Qd1 gxf4 29. exf4 Bc8 30. Qb3 Ba6 31. Re2 Nh4 32. Re3 Bc8 33. Qc2 { Key: Mate threats on the h- and g-files (engine -10). Johner resigned. } 33... Bxh3 34. Bxe4 Bf5 35. Bxf5 Nxf5 36. Re2 h4 37. Rgg2 hxg3+ 38. Kg1 Qh3 39. Ne3 Nh4 40. Kf1 Re8 0-1

[Event "Copenhagen - 'The Immortal Zugzwang Game'"]
[Site "Copenhagen"]
[Date "1923"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Friedrich Saemisch"]
[Black "Aron Nimzowitsch"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "E17"]
[Themes "zugzwang,positional,prophylaxis,immortal"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "Queen's Indian Defense: Classical Variation, Traditional Variation, Nimzowitsch Line"]
[Why "With queens, rooks and bishops still on the board, Black's 25...h6!! leaves White without a move that does not lose material: engine (4 s) gives -4.8 to -6.3 for every reply (26.Kh2 R5f3; 26.g4 R5f3; 26.Bc1 Bxb1; 26.Rc1 Re2). The game also teaches how to gain space on both wings (11...b5, 16...f5, 17...b4) and when to give a knight for a rook's invasion (21.Qxh5 Rxf2!). Paralysis can be an attacking goal; the side with nothing to move loses even with equal material."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nf3 b6 4. g3 Bb7 5. Bg2 Be7 6. Nc3 O-O 7. O-O d5 8. Ne5 { Key: Black gains queenside space and a strong bishop on the long diagonal. } 8... c6 9. cxd5 cxd5 10. Bf4 a6 11. Rc1 b5 12. Qb3 Nc6 13. Nxc6 Bxc6 14. h3 Qd7 15. Kh2 Nh5 { Key: The knight is driven to b1; Black's bishop takes the a6-f1 diagonal. } 16. Bd2 f5 17. Qd1 b4 18. Nb1 Bb5 19. Rg1 { Key: White was already worse (engine -2.3); the knight is given for the f2 pawn and the second rank. } 19... Bd6 20. e4 fxe4 21. Qxh5 Rxf2 22. Qg5 { Key: Zugzwang. Saemisch resigned. } 22... Raf8 23. Kh1 R8f5 24. Qe3 Bd3 25. Rce1 h6 0-1

[Event "St Petersburg (Chigorin Memorial)"]
[Site "St Petersburg"]
[Date "1909"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Akiba Rubinstein"]
[Black "Emanuel Lasker"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "D32"]
[Themes "endgame,rook-endgame,calculation,classic"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Traditional Variation"]
[Why "Rubinstein beats the World Champion with one tactical idea and one technical idea. The tactic: after Lasker's 16...Rxe3? (engine +1.4) comes 17.Rxc6+! bxc6 18.Qc1!, a double attack on the e3 rook and the c6 pawn. The technique: a rook ending a pawn up in which the rook cuts the black king along the sixth rank (27.Ra5, 28.Ra6), the king walks to f5, the e-pawn advances, and 40.a3! leaves Black in zugzwang. In rook endings, activate rook and king first, then push the passed pawn."]

1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. c4 e6 4. Bg5 c5 5. cxd5 exd5 6. Nc3 cxd4 7. Nxd4 Nc6 8. e3 Be7 9. Bb5 Bd7 10. Bxf6 Bxf6 11. Nxd5 { Key: Lasker's combination is unsound. } 11... Bxd4 12. exd4 Qg5 13. Bxc6 Bxc6 14. Ne3 O-O-O 15. O-O Rhe8 16. Rc1 Rxe3 17. Rxc6+ { Key: Double attack; 18...Rxd4 19.fxe3 Rd7 20.Qxc6+ Kd8 21.Rf4! and the rook enters with tempo. } 17... bxc6 18. Qc1 Rxd4 19. fxe3 Rd7 20. Qxc6+ Kd8 21. Rf4 f5 22. Qc5 { Key: Queens off; White's rook is active and his king closer. } 22... Qe7 23. Qxe7+ Kxe7 24. Rxf5 Rd1+ 25. Kf2 Rd2+ 26. Kf3 Rxb2 27. Ra5 { Key: The rook on the sixth cuts off the king and ties the black rook to a7; then 29.e4, 32.Kf4, 35.e5. } 27... Rb7 28. Ra6 Kf8 29. e4 Rc7 30. h4 Kf7 31. g4 Kf8 32. Kf4 Ke7 33. h5 h6 34. Kf5 Kf7 35. e5 Rb7 36. Rd6 { Key: A waiting move; every black move loses (engine +5.6). Lasker resigned. } 36... Ke7 37. Ra6 Kf7 38. Rd6 Kf8 39. Rc6 Kf7 40. a3 1-0

[Event "St Petersburg (Chigorin Memorial)"]
[Site "St Petersburg"]
[Date "1909"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Erich Cohn"]
[Black "Akiba Rubinstein"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "D21"]
[Themes "endgame,calculation,classic"]
[Level "2"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Accepted: Gunsberg Defense"]
[Why "The classic king-and-pawn ending. Rubinstein damages White's kingside pawns (19...Bxf3! 20.gxf3), trades every piece (engine -3.5 as soon as the rooks come off at move 25) and walks his king to h3, where White's king is chained to h2. Black's pawns then advance with tempo (...e5, ...b5, ...f5, ...g5, ...h5, ...g4) while White can only shuffle. A king on h3 against a king on g1 with a weak h-pawn wins when the opponent has no spare moves; count tempi before simplifying."]

1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 c5 3. c4 dxc4 4. dxc5 Qxd1+ { Key: A queenless game from move 5; Rubinstein plays for the ending from the start. } 5. Kxd1 Nc6 6. e3 Bg4 7. Bxc4 e6 8. a3 Bxc5 9. b4 Bd6 10. Bb2 Nf6 11. Nd2 Ke7 12. Ke2 Be5 13. Bxe5 Nxe5 14. Rhc1 Rac8 15. Bb3 Rhd8 16. Nc4 Nxc4 17. Rxc4 Rxc4 18. Bxc4 Ne4 19. Ke1 Bxf3 { Key: The structure f3/f2/h2 is permanently weak. } 20. gxf3 Nd6 21. Be2 Rc8 22. Kd2 Nc4+ { Key: Into the pawn ending, which Rubinstein has judged correctly (engine -3.5). } 23. Bxc4 Rxc4 24. Rc1 Rxc1 25. Kxc1 Kf6 26. Kd2 { Key: The king reaches h3; White's king cannot leave the h2 pawn. } 26... Kg5 27. Ke2 Kh4 28. Kf1 Kh3 29. Kg1 e5 { Key: After 39.Kf1 Kh2 40.fxg3 Kxg3 the black king eats the rest (engine -35). Cohn resigned. } 30. Kh1 b5 31. Kg1 f5 32. Kh1 g5 33. Kg1 h5 34. Kh1 g4 35. e4 fxe4 36. fxe4 h4 37. Kg1 g3 38. hxg3 hxg3 0-1

