[ECO "D41"]
[Themes "endgame,rook-endgame,positional,initiative"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Semi-Tarrasch Defense, San Sebastian Variation"]
[Why "A queenless middlegame won by the rook on the seventh (16.Rc7!, engine +1.4) and an active king. Rubinstein centralises (17.Ke3, 18.Ne5), storms the kingside with g4, f4, g5 and h4 while queens are off, wins the a-pawn with the rook still on the seventh, and finishes by walking his king to g6 with mating threats. In endings with rooks and minor pieces the king is a fighting piece, and a rook on the seventh collects pawns at leisure."]

1. d4 d5 2. Nf3 Nf6 3. c4 e6 4. Nc3 c5 5. cxd5 Nxd5 6. e4 Nxc3 7. bxc3 cxd4 8. cxd4 Bb4+ 9. Bd2 Qa5 { Key: White welcomes the queen trade: his king is already central and his rooks come first. } 10. Rb1 Bxd2+ 11. Qxd2 Qxd2+ 12. Kxd2 O-O 13. Bb5 { Key: Seventh rank; 17.Ke3 Nf6 18.Ne5 Bd7. } 13... a6 14. Bd3 Rd8 15. Rhc1 b5 16. Rc7 Nd7 17. Ke3 Nf6 18. Ne5 Bd7 19. g4 { Key: A pawn storm with the king on e3, safe because the queens are gone. } 19... h6 20. f4 Be8 21. g5 hxg5 22. fxg5 Nh7 23. h4 Rdc8 24. Rbc1 { Key: The a-pawn falls; the rook stays on the seventh. } 24... Rxc7 25. Rxc7 Rd8 26. Ra7 f6 27. gxf6 gxf6 28. Ng4 Bh5 29. Nh6+ Kh8 30. Be2 Be8 31. Rxa6 Kg7 32. Ng4 f5 33. Ra7+ Kh8 34. Ne5 { Key: The king joins the mating attack (Ra8 and Nf7 are threats). Schlechter resigned. } 34... fxe4 35. Bxb5 Nf6 36. Bxe8 Rxe8 37. Kf4 Kg8 38. Kg5 Rf8 39. Kg6 1-0

