[Opening "Horwitz Defense"]
[Why "The most quoted rook ending in chess literature. Capablanca heads for it deliberately (23.h4!, 25.Qxe8+), gets his rook to the seventh (30.Rh7) and plays 35.Kg3!!, the engine's first choice (+2.3): he lets Black take c3 and f4 with check because his king reaches f6, where king, rook and the g-pawn are worth more than two pawns. In rook endings, king activity, a rook on the seventh and a far-advanced passed pawn outweigh material."]

1. d4 e6 2. Nf3 f5 3. c4 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. Nc3 O-O 6. e3 b6 7. Bd3 Bb7 8. O-O Qe8 9. Qe2 Ne4 10. Bxe7 Nxc3 11. bxc3 Qxe7 12. a4 Bxf3 13. Qxf3 Nc6 14. Rfb1 Rae8 15. Qh3 Rf6 16. f4 Na5 17. Qf3 d6 18. Re1 Qd7 19. e4 fxe4 20. Qxe4 g6 21. g3 Kf8 22. Kg2 Rf7 23. h4 { Key: Capablanca trades into the ending with the h-pawn ready to open the h-file. } 23... d5 24. cxd5 exd5 25. Qxe8+ Qxe8 26. Rxe8+ Kxe8 27. h5 Rf6 28. hxg6 hxg6 29. Rh1 { Key: The rook reaches the seventh rank. } 29... Kf8 30. Rh7 Rc6 31. g4 { Key: The last minor pieces go; the pure rook ending begins. } 31... Nc4 32. g5 Ne3+ 33. Kf3 Nf5 34. Bxf5 gxf5 35. Kg3 { Key: Two pawns down, but the king on f6 and the rook on h7 create mating threats and support g7. } 35... Rxc3+ 36. Kh4 Rf3 37. g6 Rxf4+ 38. Kg5 Re4 39. Kf6 Kg8 40. Rg7+ { Key: The king collects everything; 52.d6 and Tartakower resigned. } 40... Kh8 41. Rxc7 Re8 42. Kxf5 Re4 43. Kf6 Rf4+ 44. Ke5 Rg4 45. g7+ Kg8 46. Rxa7 Rg1 47. Kxd5 Rc1 48. Kd6 Rc2 49. d5 Rc1 50. Rc7 Ra1 51. Kc6 Rxa4 52. d6 1-0

