[Level "3"]
[Opening "Nimzo-Indian Defense: Rubinstein System"]
[Why "The model game for the Nimzo-Indian central pawn majority against the doubled c-pawns. Botvinnik gives up the a-pawn without a fight because the pawn centre (e4, e5, f5, e6) is worth more than a flank pawn; the knight re-route 17.Ng3, the rook exchange on e1 and 26.Re6!! prepare the famous finish 30.Ba3!! Qxa3 31.Nh5+! gxh5 32.Qg5+ Kf8 33.Qxf6+ Kg8 34.e7, where the passed pawn and the king without a safe square decide (engine +5.2 after 30.Ba3). A mobile central majority is a weapon; a doubled pawn is irrelevant if it buys time."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e3 d5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 c5 7. cxd5 exd5 8. Bd3 O-O 9. Ne2 b6 10. O-O Ba6 { Key: Capablanca trades White's good bishop; the position is balanced. } 11. Bxa6 Nxa6 12. Bb2 Qd7 13. a4 { Key: Black wins the a-pawn; White gets the full centre. } 13... Rfe8 14. Qd3 c4 15. Qc2 Nb8 16. Rae1 Nc6 17. Ng3 Na5 18. f3 Nb3 19. e4 Qxa4 20. e5 { Key: The e6 pawn cuts the board in two and the queen enters. } 20... Nd7 21. Qf2 g6 22. f4 f5 23. exf6 Nxf6 24. f5 Rxe1 25. Rxe1 Re8 26. Re6 Rxe6 27. fxe6 Kg7 28. Qf4 { Key: Deflection, knight sacrifice, passed pawn. } 28... Qe8 29. Qe5 Qe7 30. Ba3 Qxa3 31. Nh5+ gxh5 32. Qg5+ Kf8 33. Qxf6+ Kg8 34. e7 Qc1+ { Key: The king walks out of the checks; e8=Q follows. Capablanca resigned. } 35. Kf2 Qc2+ 36. Kg3 Qd3+ 37. Kh4 Qe4+ 38. Kxh5 Qe2+ 39. Kh4 Qe4+ 40. g4 Qe1+ 41. Kh5 1-0

