[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Orthodox Defense, Main Line"]
[Why "The standard illustration of hanging pawns and of the passed pawn that must be 'kept under lock and key' (Nimzowitsch's phrase about this game). Capablanca turns the hanging pair c5/d5 into a protected passed pawn with 15...c4!, pushes it to c3 where it ties every white piece, and when Bernstein finally wins it (27.Nxc3?) the back rank is undefended: 29...Qb2!! (30.Qxb2 Rd1#; otherwise -5.6). Hanging pawns are a dynamic asset; back-rank weakness decides heavy-piece positions."]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 Be7 5. Bg5 O-O 6. e3 Nbd7 7. Rc1 b6 8. cxd5 exd5 9. Qa4 { Key: Black accepts hanging pawns on c5 and d5 for open files and active pieces. } 9... Bb7 10. Ba6 Bxa6 11. Qxa6 c5 12. Bxf6 Nxf6 13. dxc5 bxc5 14. O-O { Key: Engine prefers 15...Rab8, but the plan is the lesson: the pair becomes a passed pawn after 19.bxc4 dxc4. } 14... Qb6 15. Qe2 c4 16. Rfd1 Rfd8 17. Nd4 { Key: The pawn on c3, protected by the knight, blocks the c-file for White's rooks. } 17... Bb4 18. b3 Rac8 19. bxc4 dxc4 20. Rc2 Bxc3 21. Rxc3 Nd5 22. Rc2 c3 23. Rdc1 { Key: Queen attacks rook and back rank: 30.Qxb2 Rd1#; 30.Rc2 Qb1+ 31.Qf1 Qxc2; 30.Rc8 Qa1+ 31.Qf1 Qxf1+ 32.Kxf1 Rxc8. Bernstein resigned. } 23... Rc5 24. Nb3 Rc6 25. Nd4 Rc7 26. Nb5 Rc5 27. Nxc3 Nxc3 28. Rxc3 Rxc3 29. Rxc3 Qb2 0-1

