[Why "BONUS, outside the ~1950 cut-off: the companion plan to the minority attack in the same Carlsbad structure. Botvinnik plays f2-f3 and e3-e4 (9.Nge2!, 12.Kh1, 13.f3!, 16.Ng3, 20.e4!), turning the structure into a central pawn roller and a kingside attack (22.e5, 24.Nd6, 26.Qh4). Keres's 11...Bd6? and 13...Be7 lose two tempi, which is why the plan works so cleanly. Every modern treatment of the Exchange QGD teaches both plans from this game and Smyslov-Keres 1948."]

1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 d5 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bg5 Be7 6. e3 O-O 7. Bd3 Nbd7 8. Qc2 Re8 9. Nge2 { Key: The knight goes to e2, not f3, so that f2-f3 and e3-e4 become possible. } 9... Nf8 10. O-O c6 11. Rab1 { Key: Botvinnik switches from the b-pawn plan to the e4 plan; Black has wasted two bishop moves. } 11... Bd6 12. Kh1 Ng6 13. f3 Be7 14. Rbe1 Nd7 15. Bxe7 { Key: The central majority is mobile (engine +1.4). } 15... Rxe7 16. Ng3 Nf6 17. Qf2 Be6 18. Nf5 Bxf5 19. Bxf5 Qb6 20. e4 dxe4 21. fxe4 Rd8 22. e5 { Key: Knight and queen attack the dark squares around the king (engine +3.0 after 26.Qh4). } 22... Nd5 23. Ne4 Nf8 24. Nd6 Qc7 25. Be4 Ne6 26. Qh4 g6 27. Bxd5 cxd5 28. Rc1 Qd7 29. Rc3 Rf8 30. Nf5 Rfe8 31. Nh6+ { Key: A piece is won. Keres resigned. } 31... Kf8 32. Qf6 Ng7 33. Rcf3 Rc8 34. Nxf7 Re6 35. Qg5 Nf5 36. Nh6 Qg7 37. g4 1-0

