[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Orthodox Defense, Botvinnik Variation"]
[Why "The model attack with the isolated queen's pawn. The IQP gives White the e5 square and open files, so the pieces go Ne5, Qd3-h3, Rad1, and the f-pawn runs f4-f5 to open the f-file. After 18.f5! exf5 19.Rxf5 Qd6 20.Nxf7! Rxf7 21.Bxf6 Bxf6 22.Rxd5 the tactics on f7 and d5 play themselves (engine +5.7). With an IQP, attack before the opponent exchanges pieces; every exchange favours the side playing against the pawn."]

1. c4 e6 2. Nf3 d5 3. d4 Nf6 4. Bg5 Be7 5. Nc3 O-O 6. e3 Nbd7 7. Bd3 c5 8. O-O { Key: The IQP on d4 with open c- and e-files. } 8... cxd4 9. exd4 dxc4 10. Bxc4 Nb6 11. Bb3 Bd7 12. Qd3 { Key: Knight on e5, queen on h3: the classic IQP attacking set-up (engine +1.5). } 12... Nbd5 13. Ne5 Bc6 14. Rad1 Nb4 15. Qh3 Bd5 16. Nxd5 Nbxd5 17. f4 { Key: The f-file opens; e6 is gone and f7 is weak. } 17... Rc8 18. f5 exf5 19. Rxf5 Qd6 { Key: Pins on the f-file and the seventh rank; Vidmar resigned (engine +6.5). } 20. Nxf7 Rxf7 21. Bxf6 Bxf6 22. Rxd5 Qc6 23. Rd6 Qe8 24. Rd7 1-0

[Event "World Championship match, St Louis, game 9"]
[Site "St Louis"]
[Date "1886"]
[Round "9"]
[White "Johannes Zukertort"]
[Black "Wilhelm Steinitz"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "D26"]
[Themes "iqp,blockade,positional,world-championship"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Vienna Variation, Quiet Variation"]
[Why "The first great demonstration of playing against the isolated d-pawn. Steinitz blockades on d5 (10...Nb6, 11...Nbd5), exchanges minor pieces (17...Nxc3, 19...Nd5, 20...Qxe7), and when the pawn becomes a hanging pair (22.c4) attacks it with every piece. Zukertort's kingside attack (25.Rh3) achieves nothing and the game ends in a tactical collapse against the exposed white king (33...Rxc5!, 35...Qf4!, 37...Rc8). Against the IQP exchange pieces, blockade the square in front, and strike when the attacker over-extends."]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 dxc4 5. e3 c5 { Key: White takes an IQP voluntarily; the game tests whether it is a strength or a weakness. } 6. Bxc4 cxd4 7. exd4 Be7 8. O-O O-O 9. Qe2 Nbd7 10. Bb3 Nb6 { Key: The blockade on d5; 12.Bg3 Qa5 13.Rac1 Bd7 14.Ne5 Rfd8. } 11. Bf4 Nbd5 12. Bg3 Qa5 13. Rac1 Bd7 14. Ne5 Rfd8 15. Qf3 Be8 16. Rfe1 Rac8 17. Bh4 { Key: Exchanges; White now has hanging pawns on c4/d4 and no attack. } 17... Nxc3 18. bxc3 Qc7 19. Qd3 Nd5 20. Bxe7 Qxe7 21. Bxd5 Rxd5 22. c4 Rdd8 23. Re3 { Key: The rook lift is repelled; the queen returns to the centre. } 23... Qd6 24. Rd1 f6 25. Rh3 h6 26. Ng4 Qf4 27. Ne3 Ba4 28. Rf3 Qd6 29. Rd2 Bc6 30. Rg3 { Key: Back-rank and pin tactics flow from the better structure (engine -3.4 before 33.c5). Zukertort resigned. } 30... f5 31. Rg6 Be4 32. Qb3 Kh7 33. c5 Rxc5 34. Rxe6 Rc1+ 35. Nd1 Qf4 36. Qb2 Rb1 37. Qc3 Rc8 38. Rxe4 Qxe4 0-1

[Event "London (international tournament)"]
[Site "London"]
[Date "1883"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Berthold Englisch"]
[Black "Wilhelm Steinitz"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "C60"]
[Themes "bishop-pair,endgame,positional,classic"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez: Fianchetto Defense"]
[Why "The classic lesson on two bishops in the ending. After an early queen trade Steinitz keeps bishop pair against bishop and knight, places his pawns to take squares from the knight (17...b6, 19...c5, 22...g5, 25...f5, 27...a5, 28...a4), exchanges one bishop at the right moment (31...Bg5!, engine -2.3) to reach a bishop against a knight with no squares, and wins the king-and-pawn ending that follows. Steinitz's own notes on this game became the standard explanation of why two bishops beat bishop and knight."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 g6 4. d4 exd4 5. Nxd4 Bg7 6. Be3 Nf6 7. Nc3 O-O 8. O-O Ne7 9. Qd2 { Key: Black has the bishop pair; White's knight has no outpost. } 9... d5 10. exd5 Nexd5 11. Nxd5 Qxd5 12. Be2 Ng4 13. Bxg4 Bxg4 14. Nb3 Qxd2 15. Nxd2 Rad8 16. c3 Rfe8 17. Nb3 b6 { Key: The pawns start taking squares (d4, c4) from the knight. } 18. h3 Be6 19. Rfd1 c5 20. Bg5 { Key: The kingside pawns are mobile and the king centralised. } 20... f6 21. Bf4 Kf7 22. f3 g5 23. Rxd8 Rxd8 24. Be3 h6 25. Re1 f5 26. f4 Bf6 27. g3 { Key: The knight on c1 is dominated; the bishops control both wings. } 27... a5 28. Nc1 a4 29. a3 Bc4 30. Kf2 { Key: Into a won pawn ending; after 43...Kg3 the f-pawn queens (engine -14). Englisch resigned. } 30... gxf4 31. Bxf4 Bg5 32. Bxg5 hxg5 33. Ke3 Kf6 34. h4 gxh4 35. gxh4 Re8+ 36. Kf2 Rxe1 37. Kxe1 Ke5 38. Ne2 Bxe2 39. Kxe2 Kf4 40. c4 Kg4 41. Ke3 f4+ 42. Ke4 f3 43. Ke3 Kg3 0-1

[Event "Match, Cologne, game 4"]
[Site "Cologne"]
[Date "1911"]
[Round "4"]
[White "Siegbert Tarrasch"]
[Black "Carl Schlechter"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C90"]
[Themes "opposite-bishops,attack,positional,initiative"]
[Level "4"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez: Morphy Defense, Steinitz Deferred"]
[Why "Opposite-coloured bishops in the middlegame favour the attacker. After 21.Bxe5 Bxe5 White has a light-squared bishop against Black's dark-squared one, and Tarrasch uses the squares Black's bishop can never defend: the exchange sacrifice 23.Rxf7! (engine +1.3) plants the bishop on f7, the rook goes to the c-file, the king walks to g3 and the pawns f4-f5 and g4-g5 finish. With opposite bishops, count the attackers on the colour the defender's bishop cannot cover; 'drawish' applies only to the pure ending. Rogozenco's Chess Classics uses this game for the theme."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O d6 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 Be7 8. a4 { Key: White gets the bishop pair and the a-file. } 8... Rb8 9. axb5 axb5 10. c3 O-O 11. d4 exd4 12. cxd4 Bg4 13. Nc3 Nb4 14. Bf4 Nd7 15. h3 Bxf3 16. Qxf3 c5 17. Nd5 { Key: The opposite-coloured bishops appear; White's bishop on d5 hits f7. } 17... Nxd5 18. Bxd5 cxd4 19. Ra7 Ne5 20. Qb3 Bf6 21. Bxe5 Bxe5 22. g3 { Key: The bishop on f7 is untouchable; Black's bishop cannot fight for light squares. } 22... Qb6 23. Rxf7 Kh8 24. Kg2 Rxf7 25. Bxf7 Qc5 26. f4 { Key: King and pawns join; Black's pieces are tied to the light squares. } 26... Bf6 27. Re2 Qc1 28. Qd3 Qc7 29. Bb3 g6 30. Rc2 Qd7 31. g4 Rf8 32. g5 Bg7 33. Kg3 Qa7 34. Rc1 h6 35. h4 { Key: The dark-squared bishop is removed and mate on g7 follows (engine +7.2). Schlechter resigned. } 35... h5 36. f5 gxf5 37. exf5 Re8 38. f6 Bxf6 39. gxf6 Qd7 40. Qg6 1-0

[Event "New York (Manhattan Chess Club tournament)"]
[Site "New York"]
[Date "1918"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Jose Raul Capablanca"]
[Black "Frank Marshall"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C89"]
[Themes "defence,calculation,classic,initiative"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "Ruy Lopez: Marshall Attack, Original Marshall Attack"]
[Why "Marshall had prepared his gambit for years; Capablanca met it over the board and defended by the rules he later wrote down: accept the material, give some back to break the attack (16.Re2!, 19.Rxf2!), walk the king to safety (20.Ke2, 23.Kd3, 24.Kc2) and only then counterattack (26.Bd5!, 28.b4!, 31.Ra6!). Engine: White keeps about +1 from move 15 on. A prepared attack is not a refutation; calculation and the willingness to return material defend most sacrificial attacks."]

1. e4 e5 2. Nf3 Nc6 3. Bb5 a6 4. Ba4 Nf6 5. O-O Be7 6. Re1 b5 7. Bb3 O-O 8. c3 d5 { Key: The Marshall Attack: a pawn for development and mating threats on h2. } 9. exd5 Nxd5 10. Nxe5 Nxe5 11. Rxe5 Nf6 12. Re1 Bd6 13. h3 Ng4 14. Qf3 Qh4 15. d4 { Key: Capablanca returns the exchange to kill the attack. } 15... Nxf2 16. Re2 Bg4 17. hxg4 Bh2+ 18. Kf1 Bg3 19. Rxf2 Qh1+ { Key: The king is safe on c2; Black has no more pieces to bring. } 20. Ke2 Bxf2 21. Bd2 Bh4 22. Qh3 Rae8+ 23. Kd3 Qf1+ 24. Kc2 Bf2 25. Qf3 Qg1 26. Bd5 { Key: The counterattack: a passed b-pawn and the rook enters via a6. } 26... c5 27. dxc5 Bxc5 28. b4 Bd6 29. a4 a5 30. axb5 axb4 31. Ra6 bxc3 32. Nxc3 Bb4 33. b6 { Key: 36...Rxf7 37.b8=Q+ and mate in 5. Marshall resigned. } 33... Bxc3 34. Bxc3 h6 35. b7 Re3 36. Bxf7+ 1-0

[Event "St Petersburg 1895/96 (played January 1896, round 4 of the quadrangular)"]
[Site "St Petersburg"]
[Date "1896"]
[Round "4"]
[White "Harry Nelson Pillsbury"]
[Black "Emanuel Lasker"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "D50"]
[Themes "attack,sacrifice,calculation,immortal"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Pseudo-Tarrasch Variation, Primitive Pillsbury Variation"]
[Why "Lasker's most famous combination: with kings on opposite wings he plays 17...Rxc3!! and 18...Ra3!!, offering both rooks to open the a- and b-files. Engine: the combination is sound but not decisive (-0.8 after 18...Ra3); the game was decided by Pillsbury's 26.Kb2? (26.Kb1 holds, +1.2), after which 26...Rxa3! drags the king out to be mated on a5. In opposite-side castling, open the files in front of the king even at the price of rooks and calculate the hunt to the end; a defender who steps onto the wrong square loses at once."]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 c5 5. Bg5 cxd4 6. Qxd4 Nc6 7. Qh4 { Key: Opposite-side castling; both queens are already near the enemy king. } 7... Be7 8. O-O-O Qa5 9. e3 Bd7 10. Kb1 h6 11. cxd5 exd5 12. Nd4 O-O 13. Bxf6 { Key: Engine: White was already worse (-1.0) before 17.f5; the first rook sacrifice removes the c3 knight and opens the c-file. } 13... Bxf6 14. Qh5 Nxd4 15. exd4 Be6 16. f4 Rac8 17. f5 Rxc3 18. fxe6 { Key: The second rook offer: 19.bxa3 Qb6+ 20.Bb5 Qxb5+ 21.Ka1 and the queen and rook invade (engine -0.8, still a fight). } 18... Ra3 19. exf7+ { Key: 26.Kb1! was the defence; now the king is dragged out (engine -3.6). } 19... Rxf7 20. bxa3 Qb6+ 21. Bb5 Qxb5+ 22. Ka1 Rc7 23. Rd2 Rc4 24. Rhd1 Rc3 25. Qf5 Qc4 26. Kb2 Rxa3 27. Qe6+ { Key: King walk to a5 and mate. } 27... Kh7 28. Kxa3 Qc3+ 29. Ka4 b5+ 30. Kxb5 Qc4+ 31. Ka5 Bd8+ 32. Qb6 Bxb6# 0-1

[Event "Cambridge Springs (round 3)"]
[Site "Cambridge Springs"]
[Date "1904"]
[Round "3"]
[White "Emanuel Lasker"]
[Black "William Napier"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "B72"]
[Themes "attack,defence,calculation,initiative"]
[Level "4"]
[Opening "Sicilian Defense: Accelerated Dragon, Modern Variation"]
[Why "Famous as the most complicated game of its era: Lasker's g4-g5-h4 pawn storm meets Napier's central strike 12...d5!, and from move 13 to 28 almost every piece is attacked or hanging. Lasker keeps his nerve: the queen trade 16.Qxd8! in the middle of the melee (engine +1.0), 20.Bc4! ignoring the loose rook on h1, and the exchange sacrifice 21.Bxf7/22.Bxe8 come out ahead. In a tactical storm calculate concrete lines, trade queens when your king is the less safe, and value the initiative over material."]

1. e4 c5 2. Nc3 Nc6 3. Nf3 g6 4. d4 cxd4 5. Nxd4 Bg7 6. Be3 d6 7. h3 Nf6 8. g4 { Key: Napier opens the centre while White's king is on e1: the right reaction to a wing attack. } 8... O-O 9. g5 Ne8 10. h4 Nc7 11. f4 e5 12. Nde2 d5 13. exd5 { Key: Lasker trades queens into chaos; both sides have hanging pieces. } 13... Nd4 14. Nxd4 Nxd5 15. Nf5 Nxc3 16. Qxd8 Rxd8 17. Ne7+ Kh8 18. h5 Re8 { Key: When the dust settles White's rook sits on the seventh and Black's knight and bishop are loose. } 19. Bc5 gxh5 20. Bc4 exf4 21. Bxf7 Ne4 22. Bxe8 Bxb2 23. Rb1 Bc3+ 24. Kf1 Bg4 25. Bxh5 Bxh5 26. Rxh5 Ng3+ 27. Kg2 Nxh5 28. Rxb7 a5 29. Rb3 { Key: The knight is stranded and h7 falls (engine +5.2). Napier resigned. } 29... Bg7 30. Rh3 Ng3 31. Kf3 Ra6 32. Kxf4 Ne2+ 33. Kf5 Nc3 34. a3 Na4 35. Be3 1-0

[Event "Zurich"]
[Site "Zurich"]
[Date "1934"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Max Euwe"]
[Black "Emanuel Lasker"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "D64"]
[Themes "defence,calculation,initiative"]
[Level "4"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Orthodox Defense, Rubinstein Variation"]
[Why "Lasker at 65 against the future World Champion. He accepts a passive but solid position, then uses the knights (23...Nd5!, 27...Nb4!, 34...Nc2!) to create counterplay on the queenside while Euwe attacks on the kingside (29.h4, 30.h5). When Euwe overreaches, 35...Qxe5! and 36...Qxf6! give the queen for rook and knight plus an unstoppable initiative, and two knights and a rook hunt the white king. Defend actively with counter-threats, and know when a queen sacrifice turns defence into attack."]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Bg5 Nbd7 5. e3 c6 6. Nf3 Be7 7. Qc2 O-O 8. a3 Re8 9. Rc1 dxc4 10. Bxc4 Nd5 11. Bxe7 Qxe7 12. Ne4 { Key: Euwe gains space; Lasker's set-up is cramped but has no weaknesses. } 12... N5f6 13. Ng3 c5 14. O-O cxd4 15. Nxd4 Nb6 16. Ba2 Rb8 17. e4 Rd8 18. Rfd1 Bd7 19. e5 Ne8 20. Bb1 g6 21. Qe4 Ba4 22. b3 Bd7 23. a4 Nd5 { Key: Knights and rooks find squares; the queen on g5 defends and attacks. } 24. Bd3 Rbc8 25. Bc4 Bc6 26. Nxc6 bxc6 27. Rd3 Nb4 28. Rf3 Rc7 29. h4 Rcd7 30. h5 Qg5 31. Re1 { Key: Invasion on the second rank and the knight-fork motif (engine -1.5). } 31... Rd4 32. hxg6 hxg6 33. Qe2 Rd2 34. Qf1 Nc2 35. Ne4 { Key: Queen for rook and knight; Black's pieces dominate and the white king is exposed. } 35... Qxe5 36. Nf6+ Qxf6 37. Rxf6 Nxf6 38. Rc1 Ne4 39. Be2 Nd4 40. Bf3 { Key: A mating net with rook and knights; Euwe resigned. } 40... Nxf2 41. Qc4 Nd3 42. Rf1 Ne5 43. Qb4 Nexf3+ 44. gxf3 Ne2+ 45. Kh2 Nf4+ 46. Kh1 R2d4 47. Qe7 Kg7 48. Qc7 R8d5 49. Re1 Rg5 50. Qxc6 Rd8 0-1

[Event "World Championship match, Berlin, game 10 (last game)"]
[Site "Berlin"]
[Date "1910"]
[Round "10"]
[White "Emanuel Lasker"]
[Black "Carl Schlechter"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "D94"]
[Themes "defence,calculation,world-championship,initiative"]
[Level "5"]
[Opening "Slav Defense: Quiet Variation"]
[Why "Lasker had to win the last game to keep his title; Schlechter, a draw from the championship, nevertheless played for a win (7...Na6, 14...Nh5?!). The result is one of the wildest championship games: Lasker's king stays in the centre, the position is balanced as late as move 39 (engine 0.00), and Schlechter's later queen manoeuvres let Lasker reach a won queen ending and then rook against knight. Lessons: fighting with the king exposed, the practical difference between 'playing for a draw' and 'playing safely', and the technique of queen and rook against queen and knight."]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 c6 3. Nf3 Nf6 4. e3 g6 5. Nc3 Bg7 6. Bd3 O-O 7. Qc2 { Key: Schlechter chooses the sharp line; the queenside is already unbalanced. } 7... Na6 8. a3 dxc4 9. Bxc4 b5 10. Bd3 b4 11. Na4 bxa3 12. bxa3 Bb7 13. Rb1 Qc7 14. Ne5 { Key: White's king will live on e1/e2 behind the open g-file. } 14... Nh5 15. g4 Bxe5 16. gxh5 Bg7 17. hxg6 hxg6 18. Qc4 Bc8 19. Rg1 Qa5+ 20. Bd2 Qd5 21. Rc1 Bb7 22. Qc2 Qh5 23. Bxg6 { Key: A piece for two pawns; both kings exposed. } 23... Qxh2 24. Rf1 fxg6 25. Qb3+ Rf7 26. Qxb7 Raf8 27. Qb3 Kh8 28. f4 g5 29. Qd3 gxf4 30. exf4 Qh4+ 31. Ke2 Qh2+ 32. Rf2 Qh5+ 33. Rf3 Nc7 34. Rxc6 { Key: Engine still gives equality at move 39; after the exchanges White's rook and knight coordinate better. } 34... Nb5 35. Rc4 Rxf4 36. Bxf4 Rxf4 37. Rc8+ Bf8 38. Kf2 Qh2+ 39. Ke1 Qh1+ 40. Rf1 Qh4+ 41. Kd2 Rxf1 42. Qxf1 Qxd4+ 43. Qd3 Qf2+ 44. Kd1 Nd6 45. Rc5 Bh6 46. Rd5 Kg8 47. Nc5 { Key: The king walks up the board; rook beats knight. Schlechter resigned. } 47... Qg1+ 48. Kc2 Qf2+ 49. Kb3 Bg7 50. Ne6 Qb2+ 51. Ka4 Kf7 52. Nxg7 Qxg7 53. Qb3 Ke8 54. Qb8+ Kf7 55. Qxa7 Qg4+ 56. Qd4 Qd7+ 57. Kb3 Qb7+ 58. Ka2 Qc6 59. Qd3 Ke6 60. Rg5 Kd7 61. Re5 Qg2+ 62. Re2 Qg4 63. Rd2 Qa4 64. Qf5+ Kc7 65. Qc2+ Qxc2+ 66. Rxc2+ Kb7 67. Re2 Nc8 68. Kb3 Kc6 69. Rc2+ Kb7 70. Kb4 Na7 71. Kc5 1-0

[Event "Cambridge Springs (round 6)"]
[Site "Cambridge Springs"]
[Date "1904"]
[Round "6"]
[White "Harry Nelson Pillsbury"]
[Black "Emanuel Lasker"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "D50"]
[Themes "attack,sacrifice,calculation,initiative"]
[Level "3"]
[Opening "Queen's Gambit Declined: Pseudo-Tarrasch Variation, Primitive Pillsbury Variation"]
[Why "Pillsbury's revenge for 1896 in the same variation: the prepared 7.Bxf6! gxf6 8.Qh4 (reportedly kept secret for eight years) leaves Black's king without a home; Lasker grabs the b-pawn (13...Qxb2) and is punished by 15.Qd3!, 16.Ne4 and 19.f4!. The exchange sacrifice 26.Rxf5+! Qxf5 27.Rf1 ends it (engine: mate in 6 after 30.Ne5). A lead in development with the enemy king in the centre is worth a pawn or two; opening preparation works even against a world champion."]

1. d4 d5 2. c4 e6 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. Nf3 c5 5. Bg5 cxd4 6. Qxd4 Nc6 7. Bxf6 { Key: The novelty: Black's kingside pawns are wrecked before the king has castled. } 7... gxf6 8. Qh4 dxc4 { Key: White ignores the pawn; the d-file and the b1-h7 diagonal are the attack's lines. } 9. Rd1 Bd7 10. e3 Ne5 11. Nxe5 fxe5 12. Qxc4 Qb6 13. Be2 Qxb2 14. O-O Rc8 15. Qd3 Rc7 16. Ne4 { Key: Every White move attacks; the king on f8 has no shelter. } 16... Be7 17. Nd6+ Kf8 18. Nc4 Qb4 19. f4 exf4 20. Qd4 f6 21. Qxf4 Qc5 22. Ne5 Be8 23. Ng4 f5 24. Qh6+ { Key: Mate in 6 (30...Kg7 31.Qf7+ Kh6 32.Ng4+). Lasker resigned. } 24... Kf7 25. Bc4 Rc6 26. Rxf5+ Qxf5 27. Rf1 Qxf1+ 28. Kxf1 Bd7 29. Qh5+ Kg8 30. Ne5 1-0

[Event "18th DSB Congress, Breslau - 'The Gold Coins Game'"]
[Site "Breslau"]
[Date "1912"]
[Round "6"]
[White "Stepan Levitsky"]
[Black "Frank Marshall"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "C10"]
[Themes "queen-sacrifice,attack,classic,immortal"]
[Level "1"]
[Opening "French Defense: Marshall Gambit"]
[Why "23...Qg3!! puts the queen on a square where it can be taken three ways, and each capture loses: 24.hxg3 Ne2#, 24.fxg3 Ne2+ 25.Kh1 Rxf1#, 24.Qxg3 Ne2+ 25.Kh1 Nxg3+ 26.Kg1 Ne2+ with a decisive material gain (all engine-confirmed). The game before the sacrifice is a lesson in development: Marshall develops with threats (9...Be6!, 13...Rae8) while Levitsky's bishop drifts (12.Bg4, 13.Bh3). Look for the square the opponent's pawns and queen 'protect': a queen that cannot be captured safely is immune."]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 c5 { Key: Marshall's own counter-gambit in the French; both sides get open lines. } 4. Nf3 Nc6 5. exd5 exd5 6. Be2 Nf6 7. O-O Be7 8. Bg5 O-O 9. dxc5 { Key: Black regains the pawn with the f-file open. } 9... Be6 10. Nd4 Bxc5 11. Nxe6 fxe6 12. Bg4 { Key: Black wins a pawn and keeps every piece active. } 12... Qd6 13. Bh3 Rae8 14. Qd2 Bb4 15. Bxf6 Rxf6 16. Rad1 Qc5 17. Qe2 Bxc3 18. bxc3 Qxc3 19. Rxd5 { Key: The bishop on h3 is taken because 23.gxh3 Nf3+ wins the queen (engine -5.2). } 19... Nd4 20. Qh5 Ref8 21. Re5 Rh6 22. Qg5 Rxh3 23. Rc5 Qg3 { Key: All three captures lose; Levitsky resigned. } 0-1

[Event "New York"]
[Site "New York"]
[Date "1924"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Richard Reti"]
[Black "Efim Bogoljubov"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "E01"]
[Themes "calculation,attack,classic,initiative"]
[Level "2"]
[Why "The brilliancy prize of New York 1924 and the best-known 'quiet killer'. After a textbook central break (12.f3!, 15.e4!, 16.c5!) Reti's pieces reach the f-file with gain of tempo, and he finishes with 24.Bf7+ Kh8 25.Be8!!: the bishop cuts the d8 rook's defence of f8, so 25...Rxe8 26.Qxf8+ Rxf8 27.Rxf8# and anything else loses the f8 bishop (engine +35). The pattern to remember is interference: a piece placed on the line between a defender and what it defends."]

1. Nf3 d5 2. c4 e6 3. g3 Nf6 4. Bg2 Bd6 5. O-O O-O 6. b3 Re8 7. Bb2 Nbd7 8. d4 c6 9. Nbd2 { Key: The f-file is opened against Black's f-pawn structure. } 9... Ne4 10. Nxe4 dxe4 11. Ne5 f5 12. f3 exf3 { Key: The central break drives the bishop back; bishops and queen all aim at f5 and f7. } 13. Bxf3 Qc7 14. Nxd7 Bxd7 15. e4 e5 16. c5 Bf8 17. Qc2 { Key: Every exchange lands a white piece on the f-file. } 17... exd4 18. exf5 Rad8 19. Bh5 Re5 20. Bxd4 Rxf5 21. Rxf5 Bxf5 22. Qxf5 Rxd4 23. Rf1 Rd8 24. Bf7+ { Key: Interference: 25...Rxe8 26.Qxf8+ Rxf8 27.Rxf8#. Bogoljubov resigned. } 24... Kh8 25. Be8 1-0

[Event "Baden-Baden"]
[Site "Baden-Baden"]
[Date "1925"]
[Round "-"]
[White "Richard Reti"]
[Black "Alexander Alekhine"]
[Result "0-1"]
[ECO "A00"]
[Themes "calculation,attack,classic,immortal"]
[Level "4"]
[Opening "Hungarian Opening: Reversed Alekhine"]
[Why "Alekhine's most celebrated combination. After a quiet opening and a repetition he deliberately avoids (20.Bh1 h5!), the rook move 26...Re3!! (engine: the only move that keeps the balance; 27.fxe3?? Qxg3+ mates in 4) starts a 16-move sequence of knight forks and discovered attacks in which every piece takes part; the final point 42...Bd5 is a double attack on f3 and b7. Reti could have held with 27.Bf3! (engine), so the game teaches both the attacker's imagination and the precision the defender needed. Alekhine's own notes in My Best Games are the classic commentary."]

1. g3 { Key: Reti's hypermodern opening; Alekhine takes the centre and develops quickly. } 1... e5 2. Nf3 e4 3. Nd4 d5 4. d3 exd3 5. Qxd3 Nf6 6. Bg2 Bb4+ 7. Bd2 Bxd2+ 8. Nxd2 O-O 9. c4 Na6 10. cxd5 Nb4 11. Qc4 Nbxd5 12. N2b3 c6 13. O-O Re8 14. Rfd1 Bg4 { Key: A draw by repetition is offered and refused; the h-pawn starts the attack. } 15. Rd2 Qc8 16. Nc5 Bh3 17. Bf3 Bg4 18. Bg2 Bh3 19. Bf3 Bg4 20. Bh1 h5 21. b4 a6 22. Rc1 h4 { Key: The rook is immune: 27.fxe3 Qxg3+ 28.Bg2 Nxe3 29.Qxf7+ Kxf7 30.bxc6 Qxg2#. Engine: 27.Bf3! was the only defence. } 23. a4 hxg3 24. hxg3 Qc7 25. b5 axb5 26. axb5 Re3 27. Nf3 { Key: Knight forks everywhere; the e2 rook falls. } 27... cxb5 28. Qxb5 Nc3 29. Qxb7 Qxb7 30. Nxb7 Nxe2+ 31. Kh2 Ne4 32. Rc4 Nxf2 33. Bg2 Be6 34. Rcc2 Ng4+ 35. Kh3 Ne5+ 36. Kh2 Rxf3 37. Rxe2 Ng4+ 38. Kh3 Ne3+ 39. Kh2 Nxc2 40. Bxf3 { Key: The bishop attacks the f3 rook and the b7 knight; a piece is lost. Reti resigned. } 40... Nd4 41. Rf2 Nxf3+ 42. Rxf3 Bd5 0-1

