[Opening "Dutch Defense: Nimzo-Dutch Variation"]
[Why "Alekhine's own favourite game. A long-range combination starting with 29...b4! (engine -5.2) produces a new queen on c1 four moves later; a second, equally deep sequence (38...Nf3+!, 40...Qe2!!, 47...Qe2!!) forces a second promotion and a won pawn ending. Along the way: the Dutch Defence plan ...e5-e4 with a knight on d3, and passed pawns plus piece activity outweighing the exchange. The engine says 47...Qxf4 also wins, but 47...Qe2!! is the artistic choice."]

1. d4 f5 2. c4 Nf6 3. g3 e6 4. Bg2 Bb4+ 5. Bd2 Bxd2+ 6. Nxd2 Nc6 7. Ngf3 O-O 8. O-O d6 9. Qb3 Kh8 10. Qc3 e5 11. e3 a5 12. b3 Qe8 13. a3 Qh5 { Key: Black seizes e4 and kingside space. } 14. h4 Ng4 15. Ng5 Bd7 16. f3 Nf6 17. f4 e4 18. Rfd1 h6 19. Nh3 d5 { Key: A pawn is won and the knight on d3 paralyses White. } 20. Nf1 Ne7 21. a4 Nc6 22. Rd2 Nb4 23. Bh1 Qe8 24. Rg2 dxc4 25. bxc4 Bxa4 26. Nf2 Bd7 27. Nd2 b5 28. Nd1 Nd3 29. Rxa5 { Key: Both rooks given for a new queen; Black is winning. } 29... b4 30. Rxa8 bxc3 31. Rxe8 c2 32. Rxf8+ Kh7 33. Nf2 c1=Q+ 34. Nf1 Ne1 35. Rh2 Qxc4 36. Rb8 { Key: A second queen sacrifice forces a second promotion. } 36... Bb5 37. Rxb5 Qxb5 38. g4 Nf3+ 39. Bxf3 exf3 40. gxf5 Qe2 41. d5 Kg8 42. h5 Kh7 43. e4 Nxe4 44. Nxe4 Qxe4 45. d6 cxd6 46. f6 gxf6 47. Rd2 Qe2 48. Rxe2 fxe2 49. Kf2 exf1=Q+ 50. Kxf1 Kg7 { Key: The pawn ending is won; Bogoljubov resigned. } 51. Kf2 Kf7 52. Ke3 Ke6 53. Ke4 d5+ 0-1

[Event "World Championship match, Zandvoort, game 26 - 'The Pearl of Zandvoort'"]
[Site "Zandvoort"]
[Date "1935"]
[Round "26"]
[White "Max Euwe"]
[Black "Alexander Alekhine"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "A90"]
[Themes "attack,pawn-structure,calculation,world-championship"]
[Level "4"]
[Opening "Dutch Defense: Nimzo-Dutch Variation, Alekhine Variation"]
[Why "Euwe's finest game of the match: a Dutch Defence in which White's central pawns (13.d5!, 17.f4, 25.e4, 27.e5, 28.e6) become the attacking force. The knight sacrifice 21.Nxf5! is engine-approved (about level, with all practical chances to White); Euwe then gives the exchange (30.Rg1 Bxg1 31.Rxg1) to keep the e-pawn, and the passed e-pawn with a knight on e6 beats two rooks, ending with the fork 47.Ne4+. Connected central passed pawns plus a knight outweigh a rook pair's counterplay."]

1. d4 e6 2. c4 f5 3. g3 Bb4+ 4. Bd2 Be7 5. Bg2 Nf6 6. Nc3 O-O 7. Nf3 Ne4 8. O-O b6 9. Qc2 Bb7 10. Ne5 Nxc3 11. Bxc3 Bxg2 12. Kxg2 Qc8 13. d5 { Key: The knight circles to e3 to hit f5 and d5. } 13... d6 14. Nd3 e5 15. Kh1 c6 16. Qb3 Kh8 17. f4 e4 18. Nb4 c5 19. Nc2 Nd7 20. Ne3 Bf6 21. Nxf5 { Key: Three pawns for the piece and the centre rolls. } 21... Bxc3 22. Nxd6 Qb8 23. Nxe4 Bf6 24. Nd2 g5 25. e4 gxf4 26. gxf4 Bd4 27. e5 Qe8 28. e6 Rg8 29. Nf3 Qg6 { Key: The queen exchange leaves the e-pawn and the knight on e6 against two passive rooks. } 30. Rg1 Bxg1 31. Rxg1 Qf6 32. Ng5 Rg7 33. exd7 Rxd7 34. Qe3 Re7 35. Ne6 Rf8 36. Qe5 Qxe5 37. fxe5 Rf5 38. Re1 { Key: The fork wins the rook (engine +5.7). Alekhine resigned. } 38... h6 39. Nd8 Rf2 40. e6 Rd2 41. Nc6 Re8 42. e7 b5 43. Nd8 Kg7 44. Nb7 Kf6 45. Re6+ Kg5 46. Nd6 Rxe7 47. Ne4+ 1-0

